IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `scja-core`
IMPORT `sgia-interpretation-time`

-- scja1-part3-div7-9.l4: Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation in force from 1 July 2024.
-- LAYER 1, Part 3, Division 7 "Matters that are non-appealable or appealable only with permission" (ss 29, 29A), Division 8 "Further arguments" (s 29B) and Division 9 "Allocation of appeals" (ss 29C to 29E).
-- The source is a plain-text rendering, not the official text. Every name begins with the section it implements.
-- Imports the keel's `scja-core` and the staged Interpretation Act time module (the 15th day of s 29B(2)(b)), copied unchanged.
-- The Fourth, Fifth and Sixth Schedules (scja3) come in as the inputs of seams 4 to 8, and are never turned into a FALSE: an unanswered Schedule is NOTHING.
-- "General Division" in these sections is read as the core's `General Division`; whether the Act's reading includes the Singapore International Commercial Court is fork K1, and a decision of that Court is NOTHING here.

§ `Part 3: General Division of High Court (continued)`

§§ `Divisions 7 to 9: appeals`

-- The scope shared by ss 29, 29A and 29C: "a decision of the General Division made in the exercise of its original or appellate civil jurisdiction". Not a section of the Act: a helper that reads the core's Decision.
-- Plain gloss: is this decision a decision of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction? The Singapore International Commercial Court is a division of the General Division (s 18A) and s 18C applies
-- the Act to it, "subject to" the modifications of ss 18D to 18M and 80(2A): whether the provision reaches its decisions is not decided here (fork K1); each section sends that case to NOTHING before it asks this helper.
-- src:2042 | 29. In the following cases, an appeal cannot be brought against a
-- src:2043 | decision of the General Division made in the exercise of its original or
-- src:2044 | appellate civil jurisdiction:
@ref ss 29, 29A and 29C scope (src:2042-2044)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`ss 29, 29A and 29C the decision is of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction` MEANS
    decision's `made by` EQUALS `General Division`
    AND decision's `nature of the proceeding` EQUALS `a civil proceeding`
    AND (decision's `in the exercise of` EQUALS `original jurisdiction` OR decision's `in the exercise of` EQUALS `appellate jurisdiction`)

§§§ `Section 29: No appeal in certain cases`

-- s 29. Plain gloss: can an appeal NOT be brought against this decision of the General Division? JUST TRUE here means "an appeal cannot be brought". (a) a written law expressly provides the decision is final or that an appeal
-- cannot be brought; (b) the Fourth Schedule specifies the case, subject to any exception in it (seam 4: scja3 answers with the exceptions applied). "In the following cases": no "only", so a case under neither is not decided (NOTHING).
-- src:2042 | 29. In the following cases, an appeal cannot be brought against a
-- src:2043 | decision of the General Division made in the exercise of its original or
-- src:2044 | appellate civil jurisdiction:
-- src:2046 | (a) a case where it is expressly provided by any written law
-- src:2047 | that the decision of the General Division is final or that an
-- src:2054 | appeal cannot be brought against the decision of the
-- src:2055 | General Division;
-- src:2057 | (b) a case specified in the Fourth Schedule, subject to any
-- src:2058 | exception specified in that Schedule.
@ref s 29 (src:2042-2058)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `sch 4 the Fourth Schedule says no appeal can be brought` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29 an appeal cannot be brought against the decision` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF decision's `made by` EQUALS `Singapore International Commercial Court` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): s 18A makes the Singapore International Commercial Court a division of the General Division and s 18C applies the Act to it subject to modifications; whether "General Division" here includes it is not said (fork K1)
        IF NOT (`ss 29, 29A and 29C the decision is of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction` decision) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a decision of the General Division made in the exercise of its original or appellate civil jurisdiction"
        IF decision's `a written law provides that it is final or that no appeal lies` THEN JUST TRUE -- (a)
        IF `sch 4 the Fourth Schedule says no appeal can be brought` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE -- (b)
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "In the following cases" (a) and (b), with no "only"; (b)'s answer from the Fourth Schedule may itself be unanswered

§§§ `Section 29A: Permission required to appeal in certain cases`

-- s 29A(1)(b), the money test. The amount in dispute or the value of the subject matter at the hearing before the General Division, excluding interest and costs, must "not exceed" $250,000 or the sum an order under (3)
-- specifies. A case with no amount or value is not decided (K2). This is seam 10: the Fifth Schedule (scja3) consumes it.
-- src:2074 | (b) subject to any exception specified in the Fifth Schedule,
-- src:2075 | where the amount in dispute, or the value of the subject
-- src:2076 | matter, at the hearing before the General Division
-- src:2077 | (excluding interest and costs) does not exceed $250,000
-- src:2078 | or such other sum as may be specified by an order made
-- src:2079 | under subsection (3);
@ref s 29A(1)(b) (src:2074-2079)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `the sum specified by an order under section 29A(3)` IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc If the President has by order under section 29A(3) specified another sum, what sum does the order specify?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29A(1)(b) the amount in dispute or value of the subject matter does not exceed the sum` MEANS
    CONSIDER decision's `amount in dispute or value of the subject matter, excluding interest and costs`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the Act does not say what a case with no amount in dispute or value of subject matter counts as
    WHEN JUST amount THEN
        CONSIDER `the sum specified by an order under section 29A(3)`
        WHEN JUST sum THEN
            IF amount AT MOST sum THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "does not exceed $250,000 or such other sum as may be specified by an order made under subsection (3)"
        WHEN NOTHING THEN
            IF amount AT MOST `the Act's figure for s 29A(1)(b)` THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "does not exceed $250,000"

-- s 29A(1). Plain gloss: is permission required before an appeal may be brought against this decision of the General Division? "In the following cases, permission is required": (a) a written law expressly provides an
-- appeal only with permission; (b) the money test, "subject to any exception specified in the Fifth Schedule" (seam 5); (c) a case specified in paragraphs 3, 4(1) and 5(1) of the Fifth Schedule, with its exceptions (seam 6).
-- No "only": a case under none of them is not decided (NOTHING), so the Act never says here that permission is NOT needed.
-- src:2064 | 29A.—(1) In the following cases, permission is required before an
-- src:2065 | appeal may be brought against a decision of the General Division
-- src:2066 | made in the exercise of its original or appellate civil jurisdiction:
-- src:2068 | (a) a case where it is expressly provided by any written law
-- src:2069 | that an appeal may be brought only with permission, or that
-- src:2070 | no appeal may be brought except with permission;
-- src:2074 | (b) subject to any exception specified in the Fifth Schedule,
-- src:2075 | where the amount in dispute, or the value of the subject
-- src:2076 | matter, at the hearing before the General Division
-- src:2077 | (excluding interest and costs) does not exceed $250,000
-- src:2078 | or such other sum as may be specified by an order made
-- src:2079 | under subsection (3);
-- src:2081 | (c) subject to any exception specified in the Fifth Schedule, a
-- src:2082 | case specified in paragraphs 3, 4(1) and 5(1) of that
-- src:2083 | Schedule.
@ref s 29A(1) (src:2064-2083)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `a written law expressly provides that an appeal may be brought only with permission, or that no appeal may be brought except with permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law expressly provide that an appeal may be brought only with permission, or that no appeal may be brought except with permission?
      `the sum specified by an order under section 29A(3)` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `sch 5 an exception in the Fifth Schedule takes the case out of s 29A(1)(b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `sch 5 the case is one specified in paragraph 3, 4(1) or 5(1) of the Fifth Schedule and no exception applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29A(1) permission is required before an appeal may be brought against the decision` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF decision's `made by` EQUALS `Singapore International Commercial Court` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): s 18A makes the Singapore International Commercial Court a division of the General Division and s 18C applies the Act to it subject to modifications; whether "General Division" here includes it is not said (fork K1)
        IF NOT (`ss 29, 29A and 29C the decision is of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction` decision) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a decision of the General Division made in the exercise of its original or appellate civil jurisdiction"
        IF `a written law expressly provides that an appeal may be brought only with permission, or that no appeal may be brought except with permission` THEN JUST TRUE -- (a)
        IF `s 29A(1)(b) the amount in dispute or value of the subject matter does not exceed the sum` decision `the sum specified by an order under section 29A(3)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
           AND `sch 5 an exception in the Fifth Schedule takes the case out of s 29A(1)(b)` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE -- (b)
        IF `sch 5 the case is one specified in paragraph 3, 4(1) or 5(1) of the Fifth Schedule and no exception applies` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE -- (c)
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "In the following cases" (a) to (c), with no "only"; (b) and (c) wait on the Fifth Schedule's answers, which may be unanswered

-- s 29A(2). For the purposes of (1), permission must be obtained (a) where a written law specifies the court from which permission must be obtained, or the court which may grant it: from that court; (b) in any
-- other case: from the court specified in the Fifth Schedule (seam 7: scja3 answers). This answers "from which court?".
-- src:2089 | (2) For the purposes of subsection (1), permission must be
-- src:2090 | obtained —
-- src:2092 | (a) in a case where any written law specifies the court from
-- src:2093 | which permission must be obtained, or the court which
-- src:2094 | may grant permission — from the specified court; and
-- src:2098 | (b) in any other case — from the court specified in the
-- src:2099 | Fifth Schedule.
@ref s 29A(2) (src:2089-2099)
GIVEN `the court a written law specifies for permission` IS A MAYBE Court @desc If a written law specifies the court from which permission must be obtained, or the court which may grant permission, which court is it?
      `sch 5 the court from which permission must be obtained` IS A MAYBE Court
GIVETH A MAYBE Court
`s 29A(2) the court from which permission must be obtained` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the court a written law specifies for permission`
    WHEN JUST c THEN JUST c -- (a)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `sch 5 the court from which permission must be obtained` -- (b); NOTHING if the Fifth Schedule does not answer

-- s 29A(2), the yes/no twin: must permission be obtained from this court? Another court's permission is not the permission the subsection requires.
@ref s 29A(2) permission must be obtained from the court (src:2089-2099)
GIVEN court IS A Court
      `the court a written law specifies for permission` IS A MAYBE Court
      `sch 5 the court from which permission must be obtained` IS A MAYBE Court
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29A(2) permission must be obtained from this court` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 29A(2) the court from which permission must be obtained` `the court a written law specifies for permission` `sch 5 the court from which permission must be obtained`
    WHEN JUST c THEN
        IF c EQUALS court THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "permission must be obtained ... from the specified court" / "from the court specified in the Fifth Schedule"
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the court is named by a written law or the Fifth Schedule, and neither answer is given

-- s 29A(3). The President may, after consulting with the Chief Justice, by order in the Gazette, specify another sum in substitution of the sum mentioned in (1)(b).
-- src:2110 | (3) The President may, after consulting with the Chief Justice, by
-- src:2111 | order in the Gazette, specify another sum in substitution of the sum
-- src:2112 | mentioned in subsection (1)(b).
@ref s 29A(3) (src:2110-2112)
GIVEN `the person is the President` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person making the order the President?
      `the President has consulted the Chief Justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the President consulted the Chief Justice?
      `the sum is specified by order in the Gazette` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the sum specified by an order in the Gazette?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29A(3) the President may specify another sum in substitution of the sum mentioned in section 29A(1)(b)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the person is the President` AND `the President has consulted the Chief Justice` AND `the sum is specified by order in the Gazette` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The President may, after consulting with the Chief Justice, by order in the Gazette, specify another sum"

§§§ `Section 29B: Further arguments before notice of appeal is filed`

-- s 29B(1). Plain gloss: does s 29B apply to this decision? A decision made by a Judge in the original or appellate civil jurisdiction of the General Division, after any hearing "other than a trial of an action":
-- a decision after a trial is outside, in the text's own words (JUST FALSE). Other scope limits are NOTHING.
-- src:2122 | 29B.—(1) This section applies to a decision made by a Judge in the
-- src:2123 | exercise of the original or appellate civil jurisdiction of the General
-- src:2124 | Division, after any hearing other than a trial of an action.
@ref s 29B(1) (src:2122-2124)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `the decision was made by a Judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the decision made by a Judge (rather than the Registrar)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29B(1) this section applies to the decision` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF decision's `made by` EQUALS `Singapore International Commercial Court` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): s 18A makes the Singapore International Commercial Court a division of the General Division and s 18C applies the Act to it subject to modifications; whether "General Division" here includes it is not said (fork K1)
        IF NOT (`ss 29, 29A and 29C the decision is of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction` decision) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a decision made ... in the exercise of the original or appellate civil jurisdiction of the General Division"
        IF NOT `the decision was made by a Judge` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a decision made by a Judge"
        IF decision's `made after the trial of an action` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "after any hearing other than a trial of an action"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 29B(2)(a) and (b), the day before which the request must be made. "before the earlier of the following": (a) the time the judgment or order is extracted; (b) the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made.
-- The 15th day is counted by the staged Interpretation Act module (a period of days from an event, s 50(a) and (b)): the day of the event is not counted and an excluded day moves the last day to the next day that is
-- not one (fork F12, assumed, not ruled). The extraction is a date in the core (the Act speaks of a time). NOTHING if the Interpretation Act module cannot say (a contrary intention appears).
-- src:2128 | (2) Before any notice of appeal is filed against a decision to which
-- src:2129 | this section applies, the Judge who made the decision may hear
-- src:2130 | further arguments in respect of the decision if any party to the
-- src:2131 | hearing, or the Judge, requests for further arguments before the earlier
-- src:2132 | of the following:
-- src:2134 | (a) the time at which the judgment or order relating to the
-- src:2135 | decision is extracted;
-- src:2139 | (b) the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made.
@ref s 29B(2) (src:2128-2139)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which dates are public holidays in the period after the decision?
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 29B(2) the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made` MEANS
    `the last day of a period of days from the event` (decision's `date made`) `the Act's number of days for s 29B(2)(b)` `the public holidays` FALSE

@ref s 29B(2) the earlier of the following (src:2132-2139)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 29B(2) the day before which the request for further arguments must be made` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 29B(2) the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made` decision `the public holidays`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the Interpretation Act module cannot count the period
    WHEN JUST day15 THEN
        CONSIDER decision's `date the judgment or order was extracted`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST day15
        WHEN JUST extracted THEN
            IF extracted LESS THAN day15 THEN JUST extracted
            ELSE JUST day15

-- s 29B(2), the yes/no twin of the date above: is the request made in time? Reading 1 (assumed, not ruled; fork F12): "before the 15th day" excludes the 15th day itself, so a request on the 15th day is too late.
-- A request on the day the judgment or order is extracted is undecided (NOTHING): the core records the date only, and the Act speaks of the time of extraction.
@ref s 29B(2) the request for further arguments is made in time (src:2128-2139)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `the date of the request` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the request for further arguments made?
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29B(2) the request for further arguments is made before the earlier of the following` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 29B(2) the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made` decision `the public holidays`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the Interpretation Act module cannot count the period
    WHEN JUST day15 THEN
        IF `the date of the request` AT LEAST day15 THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "before the earlier of the following: ... (b) the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made"
        ELSE
            CONSIDER decision's `date the judgment or order was extracted`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN JUST extracted THEN
                IF `the date of the request` LESS THAN extracted THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE
                    IF `the date of the request` EQUALS extracted THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the core records the date of extraction, not its time of day, and the Act speaks of "the time at which the judgment or order ... is extracted"
                    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "before the earlier of the following: (a) the time at which the judgment or order relating to the decision is extracted"

-- The other reading of "before the 15th day" (fork F12): the 15th day counts, so a request made on the 15th day is in time. Kept so that both outcomes are tested; the main reading above is reading 1.
@ref s 29B(2)(b) other reading, the 15th day itself counts (src:2139)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `the date of the request` IS A DATE
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29B(2) other reading: the request is made on or before the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 29B(2) the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made` decision `the public holidays`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the Interpretation Act module cannot count the period
    WHEN JUST day15 THEN
        IF `the date of the request` GREATER THAN day15 THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made"
        ELSE
            CONSIDER decision's `date the judgment or order was extracted`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN JUST extracted THEN
                IF `the date of the request` LESS THAN extracted THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE
                    IF `the date of the request` EQUALS extracted THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the core records the date of extraction, not its time of day
                    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) the time at which the judgment or order relating to the decision is extracted"

-- s 29B(2). Plain gloss: may the Judge who made the decision hear further arguments in respect of it? Before any notice of appeal is filed; if any party to the hearing, or the Judge, requests; and the request is made
-- before the earlier of (a) and (b). Each condition not met is the text's no.
@ref s 29B(2) may hear further arguments (src:2128-2139)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `the decision was made by a Judge` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a notice of appeal has already been filed against the decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a notice of appeal already been filed against the decision?
      `a party to the hearing, or the Judge, requests further arguments` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a party to the hearing, or the Judge, requested further arguments?
      `the date of the request` IS A DATE
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29B(2) the Judge who made the decision may hear further arguments in respect of the decision` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 29B(1) this section applies to the decision` decision `the decision was made by a Judge` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "after any hearing other than a trial of an action"
        IF `s 29B(1) this section applies to the decision` decision `the decision was made by a Judge` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a decision to which this section applies"
        IF `a notice of appeal has already been filed against the decision` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Before any notice of appeal is filed against a decision to which this section applies"
        IF NOT `a party to the hearing, or the Judge, requests further arguments` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if any party to the hearing, or the Judge, requests for further arguments"
        OTHERWISE `s 29B(2) the request for further arguments is made before the earlier of the following` decision `the date of the request` `the public holidays`

-- The three things s 29B(3) lets the Judge do after hearing further arguments.
DECLARE `s 29B(3) step` IS ONE OF
    `s 29B(3) affirm the decision`
    `s 29B(3) vary the decision`
    `s 29B(3) set aside the decision`
    `s 29B(3) take another step`

-- s 29B(3). After hearing further arguments, the Judge may affirm, vary or set aside the decision. "After": a "when/upon" not met (no further arguments heard) is the text's no.
-- src:2143 | (3) After hearing further arguments, the Judge may affirm, vary or
-- src:2144 | set aside the decision.
@ref s 29B(3) (src:2143-2144)
GIVEN `the Judge has heard further arguments` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Judge heard further arguments?
      step IS A `s 29B(3) step` @desc Does the Judge affirm, vary or set aside the decision, or take another step?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29B(3) the Judge may take the step` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the Judge has heard further arguments` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "After hearing further arguments, the Judge may affirm, vary or set aside the decision"
        IF step EQUALS `s 29B(3) affirm the decision` THEN JUST TRUE
        IF step EQUALS `s 29B(3) vary the decision` THEN JUST TRUE
        IF step EQUALS `s 29B(3) set aside the decision` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "may affirm, vary or set aside" has no "only"

-- s 29B(4), the yes/no twin: does the subsection apply? "If a request for further arguments has been made under subsection (2)": not made is the text's no.
-- src:2148 | (4) If a request for further arguments has been made under
-- src:2149 | subsection (2) —
-- src:2151 | (a) a notice of appeal may not be filed against the decision
-- src:2152 | until the Judge —
-- src:2154 | (i) affirms, varies or sets aside the decision after hearing
-- src:2155 | further arguments; or
-- src:2157 | (ii) certifies, or is deemed to have certified, that no
-- src:2158 | further arguments are required; and
-- src:2160 | (b) the time for filing a notice of appeal in respect of the
-- src:2161 | decision begins on the date the Judge —
-- src:2168 | (i) affirms, varies or sets aside the decision after hearing
-- src:2169 | further arguments; or
-- src:2171 | (ii) certifies, or is deemed to have certified, that no
-- src:2172 | further arguments are required.
@ref s 29B(4) (src:2148-2172)
GIVEN `a request for further arguments has been made under section 29B(2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a request for further arguments been made under section 29B(2)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29B(4) this subsection applies` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `a request for further arguments has been made under section 29B(2)` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If a request for further arguments has been made under subsection (2)"

-- s 29B(4)(a). A notice of appeal may not be filed until the Judge (i) affirms, varies or sets aside the decision after hearing further arguments or (ii) certifies, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments
-- are required. An "until" bar: the bar is encoded and the Judge's act is a fact given by the person, never assumed. JUST TRUE here means "the bar on filing is in force". Once lifted the subsection grants nothing itself.
@ref s 29B(4)(a) a notice of appeal may not be filed until (src:2151-2158)
GIVEN `a request for further arguments has been made under section 29B(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Judge has affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments?
      `the Judge has certified, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Judge certified, or is the Judge deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29B(4)(a) a notice of appeal may not be filed against the decision yet` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `a request for further arguments has been made under section 29B(2)` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If a request for further arguments has been made under subsection (2)"
        IF `the Judge has affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "until the Judge (i) affirms, varies or sets aside the decision after hearing further arguments"
        IF `the Judge has certified, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "until the Judge ... (ii) certifies, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 29B(4)(b). The time for filing a notice of appeal begins on the date the Judge (i) affirms, varies or sets aside the decision after hearing further arguments, or (ii) certifies, or is deemed to have certified, that no
-- further arguments are required. NOTHING until one of those dates is given, and NOTHING if both are (the subsection names one of two alternative events and does not say which controls).
@ref s 29B(4)(b) the time for filing a notice of appeal begins (src:2160-2172)
GIVEN `a request for further arguments has been made under section 29B(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the date the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc If the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments, on what date?
      `the date the Judge certified, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc If the Judge certified, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required, on what date?
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 29B(4)(b) the date on which the time for filing a notice of appeal begins` MEANS
    IF NOT `a request for further arguments has been made under section 29B(2)`
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "If a request for further arguments has been made under subsection (2)"
    ELSE
        CONSIDER `the date the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments`
        WHEN JUST affirmed THEN
            CONSIDER `the date the Judge certified, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST affirmed
            WHEN JUST certified THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): both events are given; the Act does not say which date controls
        WHEN NOTHING THEN `the date the Judge certified, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required` -- NOTHING-kind (silent): if neither event has happened the time has not begun, and the Act gives no other beginning

-- s 29B(5). To avoid doubt, a party to the hearing may, but is not required to, request further arguments before filing a notice of appeal.
-- src:2176 | (5) To avoid doubt, a party to the hearing may, but is not required to,
-- src:2177 | request for further arguments before the party files a notice of appeal
-- src:2178 | in respect of the decision.
@ref s 29B(5) (src:2176-2178)
GIVEN `the person is a party to the hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a party to the hearing?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29B(5) a party to the hearing may request further arguments before filing a notice of appeal` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the person is a party to the hearing` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a party to the hearing"

@ref s 29B(5) is not required to (src:2176-2178)
GIVEN `the person is a party to the hearing` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29B(5) a party to the hearing is required to request further arguments before filing a notice of appeal` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the person is a party to the hearing` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may, but is not required to, request for further arguments"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a party to the hearing"

§§§ `Section 29C: Court to which appeal is to be made`

-- s 29C(1). Subject to (2), an appeal against a decision of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction, whether under this Act or any other written law, is to be made to the Appellate Division.
-- src:2186 | 29C.—(1) Subject to subsection (2), an appeal against a decision of
-- src:2187 | the General Division in the exercise of its original or appellate civil
-- src:2188 | jurisdiction, whether under this Act or any other written law, is to be
-- src:2189 | made to the Appellate Division.
@ref s 29C(1) (src:2186-2189)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `sch 6 the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `a written law other than the Sixth Schedule provides that the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29C(1) the appeal is to be made to the Appellate Division` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 29C(2) the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` decision `sch 6 the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` `a written law other than the Sixth Schedule provides that the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Subject to subsection (2)"
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN
        IF decision's `made by` EQUALS `Singapore International Commercial Court` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether "General Division" here includes the Singapore International Commercial Court is not said (fork K1)
        ELSE
            IF `ss 29, 29A and 29C the decision is of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction` decision THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "an appeal against a decision of the General Division in the exercise of its original or appellate civil jurisdiction"
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether (2) applies is not decided, so the Appellate Division is not decided either

-- s 29C(2), the yes/no twin of the court answer below. An appeal against a decision of the General Division is to be made to the Court of Appeal "if the Sixth Schedule or any other written law so provides": the
-- Sixth Schedule's answer is seam 8 (scja3). A written law other than the Sixth Schedule is a fact the person gives.
-- src:2193 | (2) An appeal against a decision of the General Division is to be
-- src:2194 | made to the Court of Appeal if the Sixth Schedule or any other written
-- src:2195 | law so provides.
@ref s 29C(2) (src:2193-2195)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `sch 6 the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `a written law other than the Sixth Schedule provides that the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law other than the Sixth Schedule provide that the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29C(2) the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `sch 6 the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` EQUALS JUST TRUE AND (decision's `made by` EQUALS `Singapore International Commercial Court` OR `ss 29, 29A and 29C the decision is of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction` decision) THEN JUST TRUE -- the Sixth Schedule's own answer (seam 8), for a decision of the General Division or of the Singapore International Commercial Court (which the Sixth Schedule is understood to name in its para 1(f); that Schedule is scja3's and was not read for this encoding), so fork K1 does not bite here; a decision of any other court is outside s 29C and stays NOTHING below
        IF decision's `made by` EQUALS `Singapore International Commercial Court` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): s 18A makes the Singapore International Commercial Court a division of the General Division and s 18C applies the Act to it subject to modifications; whether "General Division" here includes it is not said (fork K1); the answer depends on that only where the Sixth Schedule input is not JUST TRUE
        IF NOT (`ss 29, 29A and 29C the decision is of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction` decision) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "an appeal against a decision of the General Division"
        IF `a written law other than the Sixth Schedule provides that the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `sch 6 the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the Sixth Schedule or any other written law so provides"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the Sixth Schedule's answer is not given

-- s 29C, the court to which the appeal is to be made (seam 9: scja3's Fifth Schedule and ss 73A to 74 consume it). NOTHING when the decision is outside the sections or the Sixth Schedule is unanswered.
@ref s 29C the court to which the appeal is to be made (combines s 29C(1) and (2)) (src:2193-2195)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `sch 6 the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `a written law other than the Sixth Schedule provides that the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE Court
`s 29C the court to which the appeal is to be made` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 29C(2) the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` decision `sch 6 the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` `a written law other than the Sixth Schedule provides that the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST `Court of Appeal`
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN
        CONSIDER `s 29C(1) the appeal is to be made to the Appellate Division` decision `sch 6 the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal` `a written law other than the Sixth Schedule provides that the appeal is to be made to the Court of Appeal`
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST `Appellate Division`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): s 29C(1) names the Appellate Division only for an appeal against a decision of the General Division in its original or appellate civil jurisdiction
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the Sixth Schedule or another written law sends the appeal to the Court of Appeal is not decided

-- s 29C(3). To avoid doubt, s 29C does not create any right of appeal against a decision of the General Division.
-- src:2199 | (3) To avoid doubt, this section does not create any right of appeal
-- src:2200 | against a decision of the General Division.
@ref s 29C(3) (src:2199-2200)
GIVEN `the decision is a decision of the General Division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a decision of the General Division?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29C(3) section 29C creates a right of appeal against the decision` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the decision is a decision of the General Division` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "this section does not create any right of appeal against a decision of the General Division"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a decision of the General Division"

§§§ `Section 29D: Power to transfer appeal to Court of Appeal`

-- The ways s 29D(2) says the power may be exercised.
DECLARE `s 29D(2) way` IS ONE OF
    `s 29D(2)(a) on its own motion`
    `s 29D(2)(b) on a reference by the Appellate Division`
    `s 29D(2)(c) on an application to the Court of Appeal by a party to the appeal`

-- The appeals s 29D(1) names.
DECLARE `s 29D(1) appeal` IS ONE OF
    `s 29D(1)(a) an appeal against a decision of the General Division that has been made to the Appellate Division`
    `s 29D(1)(b) an appeal made to the Appellate Division under section 29(2) or 38(2) of the Land Acquisition Act 1966`
    `s 29D(1) another appeal`

-- s 29D(1). Plain gloss: may the Court of Appeal transfer this appeal to itself? (a) any appeal against a decision of the General Division that has been made to the Appellate Division; (b) where an order under s 39A of
-- the Land Acquisition Act 1966 is in force, an appeal made to the Appellate Division under s 29(2) or 38(2) of that Act. (b)'s "where" not met is the text's no for that paragraph. The paragraphs have no "only".
-- src:2206 | 29D.—(1) The Court of Appeal may transfer the following appeals
-- src:2207 | to itself:
-- src:2209 | (a) any appeal against any decision of the General Division
-- src:2210 | that has been made to the Appellate Division;
-- src:2212 | (b) where an order under section 39A of the Land Acquisition
-- src:2213 | Act 1966 is in force, any appeal made to the Appellate
-- src:2214 | Division under section 29(2) or 38(2) of the Land
-- src:2215 | Acquisition Act 1966.
@ref s 29D(1) (src:2206-2215)
GIVEN appeal IS A `s 29D(1) appeal` @desc Which appeal: one against a decision of the General Division made to the Appellate Division, one made to the Appellate Division under section 29(2) or 38(2) of the Land Acquisition Act 1966, or another?
      `an order under section 39A of the Land Acquisition Act 1966 is in force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is an order under section 39A of the Land Acquisition Act 1966 in force?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29D(1) the Court of Appeal may transfer the appeal to itself` MEANS
    CONSIDER appeal
    WHEN `s 29D(1)(a) an appeal against a decision of the General Division that has been made to the Appellate Division` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 29D(1)(b) an appeal made to the Appellate Division under section 29(2) or 38(2) of the Land Acquisition Act 1966` THEN
        IF `an order under section 39A of the Land Acquisition Act 1966 is in force` THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where an order under section 39A of the Land Acquisition Act 1966 is in force, any appeal made to the Appellate Division under section 29(2) or 38(2)"
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "may transfer the following appeals" (a) and (b), with no "only"

-- s 29D(2). The power in (1) may be exercised (a) on the Court's own motion; (b) on a reference by the Appellate Division; (c) on an application by a party, "but such an application may only be made" (i) on the ground that
-- the appeal was not made to the Appellate Division in accordance with s 29C, or (ii) on grounds prescribed by the Rules of Court.
-- src:2224 | (2) The power in subsection (1) may be exercised by the Court of
-- src:2225 | Appeal —
-- src:2227 | (a) on its own motion;
-- src:2229 | (b) on a reference by the Appellate Division; or
-- src:2231 | (c) on an application to the Court of Appeal by any party to the
-- src:2232 | appeal, but such an application may only be made —
-- src:2234 | (i) on the ground that the appeal was not made to the
-- src:2235 | Appellate Division in accordance with section 29C;
-- src:2236 | or
-- src:2238 | (ii) on grounds prescribed by the Rules of Court.
@ref s 29D(2) (src:2224-2238)
GIVEN way IS A `s 29D(2) way` @desc How is the power exercised: on the Court of Appeal's own motion, on a reference by the Appellate Division, or on an application by a party?
      `the application is on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Appellate Division in accordance with section 29C` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an application: is it on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Appellate Division in accordance with section 29C?
      `the application is on grounds prescribed by the Rules of Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an application: is it on grounds prescribed by the Rules of Court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29D(2) the power in section 29D(1) may be exercised in this way` MEANS
    CONSIDER way
    WHEN `s 29D(2)(a) on its own motion` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 29D(2)(b) on a reference by the Appellate Division` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 29D(2)(c) on an application to the Court of Appeal by a party to the appeal` THEN
        IF `the application is on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Appellate Division in accordance with section 29C` THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE
            IF `the application is on grounds prescribed by the Rules of Court` THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "such an application may only be made (i) on the ground that the appeal was not made to the Appellate Division in accordance with section 29C; or (ii) on grounds prescribed by the Rules of Court"

-- s 29D(3). In deciding whether to exercise the power in (1), the Court of Appeal is to have regard to matters prescribed by the Rules of Court.
-- src:2242 | (3) In deciding whether to exercise the power in subsection (1), the
-- src:2243 | Court of Appeal is to have regard to matters prescribed by the Rules
-- src:2244 | of Court.
@ref s 29D(3) (src:2242-2244)
GIVEN `the Court of Appeal is deciding whether to exercise the power in section 29D(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Court of Appeal deciding whether to exercise the power in section 29D(1)?
      `the matter is prescribed by the Rules of Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the matter one that the Rules of Court prescribe for this purpose?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29D(3) the Court of Appeal is to have regard to the matter` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the Court of Appeal is deciding whether to exercise the power in section 29D(1)` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "In deciding whether to exercise the power in subsection (1)"
        IF `the matter is prescribed by the Rules of Court` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "matters prescribed by the Rules of Court"

-- s 29D(3) as a regulative rule (ruling N2: derived from the decision above). The bearer is "the Court of Appeal"; no time is stated.
DECLARE `s 29D(3) party` IS ONE OF `s 29D(3) the Court of Appeal`
DECLARE `s 29D(3) act` IS ONE OF
    `s 29D(3) has regard to the matter prescribed by the Rules of Court`
    `s 29D(3) decides without having that regard`

@ref s 29D(3) is to have regard (src:2242-2244)
GIVEN `the Court of Appeal is deciding whether to exercise the power in section 29D(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the matter is prescribed by the Rules of Court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `s 29D(3) party` `s 29D(3) act`
`s 29D(3) the Court of Appeal must have regard to the matter prescribed by the Rules of Court` MEANS
    IF `s 29D(3) the Court of Appeal is to have regard to the matter` `the Court of Appeal is deciding whether to exercise the power in section 29D(1)` `the matter is prescribed by the Rules of Court` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `s 29D(3) the Court of Appeal`
         MUST `s 29D(3) has regard to the matter prescribed by the Rules of Court`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "s 29D(3): the Court of Appeal is to have regard to matters prescribed by the Rules of Court"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- s 29D(4). To avoid doubt, Rules of Court made for (3) may prescribe different matters for the different circumstances in (2)(a), (b) and (c).
-- src:2248 | (4) To avoid doubt, Rules of Court made for the purpose of
-- src:2249 | subsection (3) may prescribe different matters for the different
-- src:2250 | circumstances mentioned in subsection (2)(a), (b) and (c).
@ref s 29D(4) (src:2248-2250)
GIVEN way IS A `s 29D(2) way`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29D(4) the Rules of Court may prescribe different matters for the circumstance` MEANS
    CONSIDER way
    WHEN `s 29D(2)(a) on its own motion` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 29D(2)(b) on a reference by the Appellate Division` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 29D(2)(c) on an application to the Court of Appeal by a party to the appeal` THEN JUST TRUE

-- s 29D(5). To avoid doubt, an appeal may be transferred under (1) even if it was made to the Appellate Division in accordance with s 29C(1).
-- src:2254 | (5) To avoid doubt, an appeal may be transferred under
-- src:2255 | subsection (1) even if it was made to the Appellate Division in
-- src:2256 | accordance with section 29C(1).
